[RUS.FullName]
Fiery Salient 
[RUS.Description]
[RUS.Epilogue]
Until July 18, 67th Guards Rifle Division held positions in the sector of Kalinovka and Novoselovka. On July 19 Soviet forces began a counteroffensive and reached the line of Gertsovka, Cherkasskoe - the line they had held before the beginning of the Battle of Kursk. 

In August, forces of the 6th Guards Army flanked the enemy at Kharkov, which contributed to the recapture of Belgorod and Kharkov. On October 15, the army was dispatched to the 1st Baltic Front, and 67th Guards Rifle Division took defensive positions in the vicinity of Nevel. Until January 1944, the unit conducted defensive battles, then it took part in destroying the enemy's Nevel Army Group. In the summer of 1944, the division liberated Vitebsk and Polotsk, took part in the Shyaulyay and Memel Offensives during the larger Baltic Offensive. The 67th Guards Vitebsk Rifle Division of the Red Banner finished the war in a series of battles with the German Courland Group.

Guards Captain Ivan Lagutin went missing on June 25, 1944 while conducting a crossing of the Daugava River by Labeiki village in Vitebsk region. Lagutin's family treasures the last letter from Ivan, dated July 24, 1944: 

How do you do, my dear? 

Yesterday, the noise of gunfire didn't cease from morning until the night. Everyone, sweating buckets, was methodically doing their work on their sectors, like in an enormous workshop, having forgotten any fear, having dropped the reins: either you'll be killed or you won't be. You cannot protect yourself in a slaughter like this anyway, and the hands do what needs to be done almost automatically. It's raining cats and dogs today. The boys are hitting the hay to get a good night's sleep. Before going to sleep, I, as usual, reached for my amulet, a cigarette holder you gave me. Sleep was driven from my eyes at once - my pocket was empty! At first, I just sat stunned, without a single thought in my head. Then, a thought came to my head. To look for it? But where?! In the deep grass, in the rain, amidst the dark of the night? 

So as I sat in my fox hole I thought that it is not so awful to die now. It would have been harder in the beginning, before I had killed my first Nazi. How many of them have I sent to hell? But I don't want to die. I don't want to now, because I survived Kursk, and the war is likely to end soon.

I'm trying to sell myself the idea that it is just luck. The amulet, if one thinks clearly, is just a symbol I've invented for myself. What's the connection between that trinket and my life and death?
[Rus_Cherkasskoe_south.FullName]
Southern outskirts of Cherkasskoe
[Rus_Cherkasskoe_south.Description]
\t Captain Ivan Lagutin fought near Moscow and at Stalingrad; was wounded in action. After recovering, he was appointed commander of a rifle battalion, 196th Guard Rifle Regiment, 67th Guard Rifle Division. Ivan has considerable battle experience and knows well the enemy's weaknesses and strengths, and skillfully leads the battalion in the field. 
\t July 5, before dawn. Yesterday the Germans began an offensive against the battle outposts in Butovo and Gertsovka. Until late at night, artillery rattled, machine guns crackled, tank motors roared. At nightfall, scarce groups of surviving soldiers and commanders who were first to face the attack, returned through our frontline. They report a multitude of German tanks attacking restricted sectors. 
\t Heavy tanks are particularly dangerous, as their front cannot be penetrated with our guns. We should emphasize on firing at the sides and engines. We should gather groups of experienced fighters from different squad sections. They will destroy any enemy tanks that break through to our positions, with Molotov cocktails and grenades. It is possible to successfully fight with fascists, the main thing is not to waver in the field.
\t Behind our backs, our women and children work without a minute of respite. They send more and more weapons and ammunition to the front, they rely on our resolve. I have just re-read the letters from my relatives. There are moments, when you pay no attention to the war - you just listen to the warbles of a nightingale. Thoughts are fleeting; they fly away so far and so fast, as if they try to outrun death. The guns will soon rattle again, and every second will be decisive. Will we stay alive or not? Will our Motherland be free? And I answer again: there will be life and freedom. When I see my soldiers and officers, my assurance grows. We will fulfill comrade Stalin's order and the dictate of the Motherland: not a step back!
\n 
\t SITUATION AND ORDER
\t The enemy has captured the battle outposts on the line of Gertsovka - Butovo and commences an offensive at dawn, July 5. 67th Guard Rifle Division under the command of Colonel Baksov makes a stand on the line of Cherkasskoe - Lukhanino. 196th Guard Rifle Regiment under the command of Bazhenov defends Cherkasskoe, denying the enemy an advance in the direction of Butovo - Yarki.
\n 
\t OPERATION INSTRUCTIONS:
\t Up to an infantry battalion with tanks of the Grossdeutschland Division advances from the south to assault Cherkasskoe. Heavy tanks of a new design have been spotted. 
Lagutin's battalion will consolidate on Cherkasskoe. Concentrate your efforts on holding the first trench. If the enemy breaks through, withdraw to the auxiliary positions on the southwestern outskirts of the village. 
\t Destroy the enemy tanks that break through to our positions at all cost, use antitank guns and rifles, grenades or any other means. Each rifle platoon should have a group of "tank destroyers". Political commissars will lay the groundwork and provide the party layer. Deploy antitank rifles to firing positions on the flanks. Their order is to destroy enemy tanks that make it to the front line with point-blank fire at the sides and rear. 
\t Mortar screen-fire will cut-off the infantry from the tanks before they reach the frontline. Concentrate machine gun fire on the most dangerous infantry groups. If the enemy comes close to your positions, meet him with suppressive fire, mass hand grenade volleys and direct fire when in sight. Enemy infantry that reaches the trenches is to be killed with grenades, point-blank fire and in close combat. 
\t Keep at least half of the antitank artillery guns and tanks in positions that allow flanking fire on the advancing tanks. Shoot the heavy tanks only at the side or rear.
\t Mortar screen-fire will cut-off the infantry from the tanks before they reach the frontline. Concentrate machine gun fire on most dangerous infantry groups. If the enemy comes close to the positions, meet him with fire for effect, mass hand grenades throwing and shoot him in sight. Enemy infantry, broken in trenches, is to be destroyed with grenades, point-blank fire and jabbing.
\t Keep at firing positions that allow flanking fire on advancing tanks at least one half of antitank artillery guns and tanks. Shoot heavy tanks only at sides or rear.

[Rus_Cherkasskoe_north.Description]
\t July 5, afternoon. Early in the morning, the Germans attempted to drive us from our positions behind the antitank ditch. The first strike was the most potent, but we held out. Bombs and shells turned the earth upside-down, but the battalion still held the field. With machinegun fire and the support of mortars we laid the enemy motorized infantry to waste on the ground by the barbed wire and killed a lot of Nazis. The enormous tank spearhead got stuck against the antitank ditch and the minefield and couldn't attack with the infantry. 
\t I remember how we dug all those trenches and holes, ditches and escarpments, dykes and pits, for months, until our calloused hands bled. All that extreme effort has paid off today. For several hours the enemy hovered in place, trying to get over the obstacles. He was crushed by our artillery, bombed several times with our attack planes. But the Germans obstinately dragged themselves through the mines and the fire. By three in the afternoon they began to flank our positions and charge with their tanks in the middle. A solid sheet of dust, smoke and ash hung in the air, and under its cover the Germans got closer and closer.
\t Soldiers and commanders fight resolutely, there are no cowards and fear-mongers in the battalion, in spite of heavy losses. We withdrew to the positions on the southern outskirt of Cherkasskoe only when orderered by the regiment commander. The village is turned over with shells and bombs, the soil is scarred with ditches, communication trenches and craters, and every square foot of the land is bruised. One can hear the groans of the wounded and the barking of hoarse commanders from every direction. Everything was clouded by a dense fog of raised dust, ashes and the stench of rent and injured human bodies. 
\t Today I've lost a lot of comrades-in-arms, men I went through the mill with, guys I shared the burden of the war with. Today they were killed by bastard fascists. They killed them because we protect the independence of our Motherland, because we do not want to live under the heel of Germany. That's why they destroy us. I'll see them dead first. I'll take three of them for every killed friend. Even if they kill me, new soldiers will come in my place, and they will take revenge for all of us. The war will be settled, either we kill them, or they kill us. And today my resolve grows: it has to be us doing the killing, there's no other way!
\n 
\t SITUATION AND ORDER
\t The 196th Guard Rifle Regiment under the command of Bazhenov, equipped with antitank guns, antitank rifles and static flamethrowers, defends the sector 1km to the west of Cherkasskoe, Hill 237.8, Yarki, denying the enemy access in the direction of Butovo - Yarki. A tank company of the 245th independent tank regiment holds ground in that sector as well. There is an antitank strong point (ATSP) in the forest 0.5km to the northeast of the villages. It consists of antitank artillery and a mortar battery. 
\t The composite battalion of the 196th Guard Rifle Regiment in a rugged defensive battle repelled several attacks of enemy tanks and infantry in the sector of the antitank ditch, and then withdrew to the village. Losses in the rifle companies are up to 60%.
\n 
\t OPERATION INSTRUCTIONS:
\t The enemy suffered heavy losses in the repelled attacks of the Grossdeutschland Motorized Division units. Up to an infantry battalion with tanks advances from the south and southwest to assault Cherkasskoe. 
\t Lagutin's composite group in conjunction with an antitank artillery battery, a tank platoon, and an antitank rifle platoon will firmly hold positions in Cherkasskoe. A special priority should be given to holding the first trench on the southwestern outskirts and the antitank strong point in the forest 0.5km to the northeast of the village. Take measures to destroy enemy tanks and armored vehicles first. In order to achieve this, organize firing positions that allow sudden strikes at the sides and rear parts of enemy tanks. Thoroughly hide your firing positions, deliver fire directly only when ordered to. 
\t Use mortars against large groups of charging infantry. If the infantry goes to the ground, cease fire, and continue firing when the enemy charges again. 
\t Antitank rifles should be prioritized to personnel carriers and armored vehicles. Plan antitank rifle flanking maneuvers to destroy advancing tanks. Use concentrated fire of at least 3-4 antitank rifles on a single target. 
\t If the enemy reaches the positions in the southern part of the village, withdraw to the trenches in the southwestern part. Hold them until the last man standing. Flank and attempt to drive the enemy out under the fire of the antitank strong point. 

\t Before the battle, remind the soldiers and commanders of the order of the People's Commissar of Defense No. 227: "Not one step back! Such should be our motto".

[Rus_Cherkasskoe_north.FullName]
Cherkasskoe 
[Rus_Syrcevo_Dubrovo_south.Description]
\t July 6, late afternoon. Yesterday at dusk the regiment left Cherkasskoe and in small groups withdrew to the new line, about three kilometers to the east of the village. All night long we dug trenches on the slopes of the hill. And all night we heard shots and saw flying rockets from the direction of Cherkasskoe. The Germans were finishing off our men. It hurts that we failed to carry the wounded out of the battlefield, but the Germans had blocked all routes, and in the afternoon their air force hunted down even single carriages. 
\t Today at dawn I looked around. A handful of soldiers, several antitank rifles and a pair of machine guns - nothing more of the battalion left. Four antitank guns planted themselves behind our backs. The Germans were in no hurry. We spotted them only several hours after dawn. We heard the droning of motors from their direction; a huge cloud of dust rose in the air, and we saw an enormous tank spearhead, crawling towards our positions. Laptezhniks hung overhead and sprayed the vicinity with bombs, not giving us a chance to raise our heads. 
\t The battalion stood at bay, but what could be done against a monstrous steel wedge concentrated on the infantry? We had to withdraw again to the northeast. We should thank the SP gunners and antitank crews for covering us, otherwise the tracks of fascist tanks would crush our bones against the ground of Kursk. By noon we reached the positions of 90th Division in the vicinity of Dubrova.
\t As we say, one that's been caned is worth two that haven't. Only the most experienced, bravest and outright luckiest are left in the service. We quickly gathered a composite battalion, reinforced it with the guys from the training battalion, and then they gave us some antitank rifles and grenades. We caught our breaths, had a snack and began taking up defensive positions on the frontline. 
\t The positions are well organized, the trenches are full-sized, we have a clear view, there is an antitank ditch, a mine field and barbed wire in front of us. Several batteries of antitank guns are around us. The Germans won't surprise us for sure. And we'll try to knock their teeth out! 
\t Yet, the fascists are strong, but they don't advance recklessly anymore, we've taught them some humility. They take their time and prepare, move with caution, their engineers look for mines, and they send strong recon vanguards. We need more cunning and skill in how we use our infantry and not just wait for the next counterattack. We need to shoot the Germans, not offer our chests to enemy bullets!
\n 
\t SITUATION AND ORDER
\t In the morning of July 6, the enemy broke through the defenses of 67th Guards Rifle Division with two tank and a grenadier regiment, and reached the vicinity of Dubrova. Units of the division withdrew to the north shore of the Pena River and to Dubrova, where 90th Guards Rifle Division stands. Now, they arrange themselves and organize defenses. 
\t The composite battalion of 196th Guards Rifle Regiment was reinforced near Dubrova and assumed the defense of an antitank strong point at Hill 241.1, together with artillery and antitank rifle units.
\n 
\t OPERATION INSTRUCTIONS:
\t A grenadier regiment of the Grossdeutschland Division, supported with several tanks, broke through our defenses and reached the line 1km to the southwest of Hill 241.1. Up to 30 tanks with infantry have massed in the passage to the west of the antitank ditch. Using engineers, artillery and air strikes, the enemy tries to create lanes through the ditch. This could possibly turn into an offensive in the next 30 minutes. 

\t Lagutin's composite group in conjunction with an antitank artillery battery and antitank rifle platoon will secure the strong point. Special priority should be given to holding the positions in front of the middle section of the antitank ditch and on the southern outskirts of Dubrovo. 
\t Antitank positions should be prepared and equipped for constant fire. Camouflage them thoroughly and cover the crews from enemy air strikes. 
\t Use APCR shells only against heavy tanks. Fire at a range of no more than 500 meters for 76mm guns, 300 meters for 45mm guns and no more than 300 meters for antitank rifles. 
\t Ensure the full cohesion of infantry, guns and antitank rifles. Cover the flanks of firing positions with infantry; deploy antitank rifles to allow them to fire at the sides of breaching tanks. 
\t Engage enemy infantry in narrow passages with volleys from the rifle squads, diagonal machine gun fire, and artillery barrages. Provide close-range hand-held and mounted machine guns to cover the junctions with small-arms fire, position them to have at least quarter overlapping fields of fire. 
\t If the enemy breaks through the first positions, withdraw to the trenches on the southern outskirts of Dubrova. Prepare counterattack lines for flank and rear attacks against breaching enemy groups. Destroy isolated tanks with antitank rifles, grenades and Molotov cocktails, allowing the artillery to concentrate on the frontline.

[Rus_Syrcevo_Dubrovo_south.FullName]
Syrtsev-Dubrova line
[Rus_Syrcevo_Dubrovo_north.Description]
\t July 7, at dawn. Our Guards army has done its damnedest, and even more. For three days the fascists have been hitting our defenses with their armored fist, but they cannot break through them. Soldiers and commanders, forming the backbone of the defense, stand resolute, and fight with skill and courage. Men put their lives on the line, many perish in violent fighting, but the enemy pays dearly for each one of them. A lot of Germans will find their resting place at Kursk, their eternal Lebensraum. 
\t At a time of universal valor it is especially shameful and disgusting to attempt saving one's life on the backs of his own soldiers. Yesterday the mortar battery commander fled from his post with the only vehicle. He came back only in the face of a military tribunal. His rambling excuses are disgraceful and pathetic. Command decided to give him a chance for atonement, but I wouldn't rely on a commander like this in the field. 
\t About half a thousand men left in the regiment. Our positions are mixed with the battle formations of the 90th Rifle Division, which had suffered heavy losses as well. We can see that command sends reinforcements day and night to hold the defense. Last night, numerous tanks deployed behind the infantry, antitank guns planted themselves on the hills. But there isn't enough infantry and there's a shortage of men in the trenches on the frontline. That's why all our hopes are centered on artillery and tanks. It's a shame, but the air force does not help much. Fascist aircraft bomb us from dawn till dusk, and though our losses in the bombing are not heavy, it is almost impossible to evacuate the wounded or bring ammunition and food during daylight.
\t Last night was the calmest since the beginning of the fighting. Sometimes gun shots and machine gun crackling faded out entirely, and a surprising silence enveloped us. You don't want to think about the war at such a moment. Death, revenge, and zealotry, everything monstrous and incomprehensible - no words could even approximately depict the universal bloody chaos, the fruit of Satan's plot. Amidst all of these, the keener realize the importance and the depth of the mundane chores of a peaceful life. 
\t In the letters to my kin I beg them to live cheerfully and stay united. Live every second of your life while you're alive, don't just vegetate. Happiness is not in mended socks and combing a child's hair. It is important, but there are even more important things: inner vigor, merriment and love.
\n 
\t SITUATION AND ORDER
\t Units of the Grossdeutschland Division reinforced with Panther tanks have forced our troops back to the northwest of Dubrovo. The enemy will possibly charge at dawn of July 7. His main attack will most likely be in the direction of command point 254.5. Up to a motorized infantry regiment with tanks advances towards Dubrova, Syrtsev. 
\t Division units stand on guard along the shore of Pena up to the northern outskirts of Syrtsev, along with units of 90th Rifle Division and 1st Mechanized Brigade. There is an antitank strong point consisting of antitank artillery and tanks in the defensive belt.
\n 
\t OPERATION INSTRUCTIONS:
\t The Panzerfuesiliere regiment of the Grossdeutschland Division, supported by tanks, advances in the direction of Syrtsev. Up to 30 tanks with infantry have gathered on the eastern shore of the Pena. Enemy attacks might begin as soon as 08:00, July 7. Enemy tank and infantry actions are supported with ground attack aircraft and artillery fire. 
\t Lagutin's composite group in conjunction with an antitank artillery battery, antitank rifle unit and the tanks of the 1st Mechanized Brigade will secure the strong point to the north of Syrtsev. \t A special priority should be given to holding the positions in Syrtsev itself. By any means you must deny the enemy the chance to occupy the brigade command post in Syrtsev. 
\t Organize antitank firing positions on the hill to the north of Syrtsev in order to fire at the sides of the enemy tanks advancing on the command post. Fire at a range of no more than 500 meters for 76mm guns, 300 meters for 45mm guns and no more than 300 meters for antitank rifles. 
\t Cover artillery firing positions with infantry in the front and on the right flank. Deploy antitank rifle units in the second trench with the order to shot at the tanks at point-blank range. Entrench the assigned tanks and cover them with infantry on the flanks thus preventing the approach of enemy anti-tank groups.
\t Do not let the enemy infantry reach the firing position on the left flank. Provide diagonal machine gun fire and artillery barrage fire in order to do this. Provide close-range hand-held and mounted machine guns to cover the right flank. 
\t If the enemy breaks through to the outskirts of Syrtsev, counterattack him decisively at his flank and rear with assigned tanks and infantry from the first positions. Do not let the enemy take the command post at any cost. Defense readiness by 06:00, July 7.
[Rus_Syrcevo_Dubrovo_north.FullName]
Defense of Syrtsev
[Rus_Verchopenye_south.Description]
\t July 8, morning. Yesterday the enemy rushed us with his beastly might again. He tried to crush and trample our defenses and rush towards Oboyan. But our army has changed significantly. I remember how just a year ago German attacks turned our regiments to dust, how units disolved to fragments of groups and squads, crawling eastward. It's all different now. Despite heavy losses, the regiment retained its command structure, the banner and the main body of soldiers and its commander, and fresh reinforcements gather around them. 
\t Our regiment does not resemble a rotten stub, that can be easily knocked down with a jack boot. The Red Army of today is like a compressed spring. While the fascists are strong, they wait for the spring to rust and crumble. But we believe that our spring will soon recoil and strike with all its accumulated rage. It seems like only moments separate us from either dying or defeating the enemy. 
\t Everyone is a hero on the frontline today. One infantry man fights as well as three; it's hard to single out any one in particular. We stand our ground together, fight not sparing ourselves, with our teeth clenched. The anti-tank gun crews work like devils, their cannons shoot like machine guns, there is not time to count the shell cases ringing. Our tankers help us greatly. Just try to hit a dug-in tank! Fearless engineers lay mines under the Germans' noses, and they do it so well, that occasionally several fascist tanks freeze motionless at the same time.
\t War is a hard trial. War reveals every trait; it shows everyone the guts of every man. War opens your eyes; it gives the proper impetus to your consciousness. And if anyone poorly understands what life is, what the Motherland is, what friendship is, war makes these things clear. 
\t I live between blood and death. I have neither anxiety nor cowardice. The war took them out of me. I flirt with death. Trenches, flying soil, shells. They have a beauty and charm of their own, as the moonlight does. I have a feeling like I'm looking at a picture and trying to peek beyond the framework to know what happens next. 
\t Now we count only on ourselves - and on Lady Luck to some extent. Yesterday a shell pierced the roof of HQ's dugout and planted itself right in the middle of it. It was a dud. Fate had mercy on six men at once. How can one be so lucky? - it's silly! It is rightfully said, that a month of war is worth several years of peace. So, we should wait and fight for victory. And after that we'll make our own luck and, maybe, arrange our lives anew.
\n 
\t SITUATION AND ORDER
\t Units of the Grossdeutschland Division have reached the valley 2km to the south of Verkhopenye, Gremyachy, with the intent to press on further along the eastern shore of the Pena on the road to Oboyan. Up to a motorized infantry regiment with tanks advances towards the southern and eastern outskirts of Verhopenye. Enemy aircraft conduct massive bombing raids. 
\t Division units together with infantry from the 90th Rifle Division, and tanks and artillery from the 1st Mechanized Brigade hold the line along the western shore of the Pena and along the northern slopes of the Verkhopenye, Gremyachy valley.
\n 
\t OPERATION INSTRUCTIONS:
\t The Panzerfuesiliere regiment from the Grossdeutschland Division, with tank support, advances in the direction of Syrtsev, Verkhopenye. Up to 50 tanks with infantry are massing in the valley on the eastern shore of the Pena. Enemy tank and infantry actions are supported with air force strikes and artillery fire. 
\t Lagutin's composite group in conjunction with an antitank artillery battery, an antitank rifle unit and tanks from the 200th Tank Brigade will secure the strong point to the southeast of Verkhopenye village. Special priority should be given to holding the positions in the vicinity of the bridges across the Pena. A firm defense should provide the conditions for a flanking counterattack against the advancing enemy in the direction of Syrtsev, Yakovlevo. 
\t The army commander has noticed that while repelling enemy tank attacks, their crews leave their vehicles after the first successful hit. The enemy, uses the ensuing chaos and pulls the tanks rearwards and repairs them quickly and then puts them back into service. 
\t Every tank must be rendered completely inoperable, damaged beyond repair or incinerated. 
\t Form a mobile defensive unit and parties of close-range tank hunters, supported with engineer assets. Equip the mentioned parties with antitank mines, grenades and Molotov cocktails.
\t Entrench the infantry, materiel and any command posts in advance. Organize decoy and provisional positions for infantry and firing positions for artillery. Deceive the enemy, dissipate his combat power with decoy targets and trenches. 
\t Artillery supports the defense from the western shore of the Pena with barrage lines on the valley and delivers concentrated fire on call to the sectors specified.
[Rus_Verchopenye_south.FullName]
Southern outskirts of Verkhopenye
[Rus_Verhopenye_north.Description]
\t July 8, afternoon. The Germans drove us out of our positions on the eastern shore of the Pena, we had to withdraw to the northern part of Verkhopenye village. Under the cover of tanks and artillery fire, German infantry leaked to our flank and rear. We have been at war for so long, that we rarely pay any attention if shells and mines blow further than 100-150m. Submachinegunners are the same thing, if they are in a small group they are even less dangerous, as automatic fire is rather ineffective. The Germans broke-in and cut off every line of retreat except for a single path along the bog. Along that path, they deployed riflemen, who were firing at us unceasingly. We jogged about 300 meters from the Germans, and not only was noone killed, nobody was even wounded. 
\t But when the aircraft, artillery, mortars, and machine guns concentrate their fire on infantry, that's a whole different story. Losses increase, especially in the wounded. The fourth day of the battle was the most difficult due to the intensity and strength of the enemy air strikes. Besides the ground engagements, our units came under violent air attacks. Today the fascists' advance is almost entirely a merit of their bombers that had literally punched passages in our defenses. In spite of this, our men did not wilt and fought resolutely for each bit of land. The infantry held its positions until afternoon. No one left their trenches even when tanks rolled over them.
\t We have too much of a mess so far. Air support is poor, especially close ground support, which, in spite of the identification signals of our forces, would occasionally drop its destructive payloads on friendly troops. 
\t There are examples of exemplary military competence. Today we saw, how our antitank artillery shot a column of German tanks on the roadway to Oboyan. As the tanks moved along the road, we decided to make them pay a toll. As soon as the leading enemy vehicles entered the engagement zones, all the crews struck at the tanks' sides simultaneously. It was a powerful and sudden attack. The Germans could not even deploy in battle order. Several tanks were obliterated, the others left the roadway and fled eastward, trying to get out of this fatal trap.
\n 
\t SITUATION AND ORDER
\t Grossdeutschland Division and 11th Tank Division direct the main northward attack along the roadway to Oboyan. A motorized regiment of the Grossdeutschland with tank support advances to the northern part of Verkhopenye village with the task of establishing a bridgehead on the western shore. Enemy tank and infantry actions are supported by bombers and ground attack aircraft. 
\t After the withdrawal, division units stand on the defensive lines along the western shore of the Pena, along with the 200th Tank Brigade and 1st Mechanized Brigade. Your mission is to prevent enemy tanks and infantry from reaching the western shore of the Pena.
\n 
\t OPERATION INSTRUCTIONS:
\t A motorized regiment from the Grossdeutschland Division with tank support advances towards the western part of Verkhopenye. Up to 20 tanks with infantry have massed on the eastern shore of the Pena. Enemy tank and infantry actions are supported with air strikes and artillery fire. 
\t Lagutin's composite group in conjunction with the tanks of the 200th Tank Brigade will secure the strong point in the western part of the village. Units, withdrawing to the defensive area, will be gathered, loaded up, and deployed in corresponding sectors. Use all the available materiel and rounds; mine the front line of defense. Destroy advancing enemy units, stand your ground firmly. Suppress panic and any defeatist attitudes decisively. 
\t Special priority should be given to holding the bridge across the Pena. Do not let the enemy consolidate on the eastern shore of the Pena and prevent his crossing to the western shore. Deploy the most experienced soldiers and commanders to outposts that will deny any sudden enemy breakthroughs to the bridge across the Pena. If the enemy reaches the western shore of the Pena, immediately begin a counterattack with all available forces and destroy the enemy. 
\t Order the machine gun crews to kill enemy tank crews trying to escape from their damaged tanks and armored vehicles. Assign the best riflemen from every squad to enemy officer killing duty. 
\t Artillery will support the defense from the western shore of the Pena with creeping barrage lines and will deliver prepared concentrated fire on specific sectors: Syrtsev, Krasnaya Dubrava, Shchenyachye area, MTS Verkhopenye. 
\t Casualties will be evacuated for treatment to Ivnya mobile hospital.
[Rus_Verhopenye_north.FullName]
Verkhopenye 
[Rus_Novoselovka.Description]
\t July 9, noon. Most of the soldiers and commanders are dead tired. We've had no rest for the last several days, men are literally ready to drop, falling asleep on their feet. There's a shortage of mechanical transport, everything must be carried. Reaction times are dulled from exhaustion and overexertion; apathy and indifference to what is going on manifest themselves. Food and hot water delivery is still badly organized. There is a particular shortage of water. Rivers and streams are filled with dead human and animal bodies, derelict vehicles and tanks, polluted with gas and oil. Wells are filled in. We were often forced to drink straight from puddles. We finally found some wells in Novoselovka and quenched our thirst. 
\t This day is special for we have received the letters from home for the first time since the beginning of the battle. We could not wait for those envelopes! There's nothing more precious in the whole world. But there is terrible news among those letters. Right now, the political instructor reads aloud one of those bitter letters.
\n 
\t \t Dear soldier of the Red Army! 
\t \t The Red Army has liberated my city, and I'm writing to you, my unnamed soldier. When our 
\t region was occupied, several German soldiers lived in our house. One of them, a boy 
\t of eighteen, stripped off all his clothes and at gunpoint forced us, women, to do the same. 
\t Amid the guffaw of other soldiers, he ordered us to sponge our bodies with cologne water. And 
\t then he raped a girl right before our very eyes. It was a nightmare that cannot be forgotten. 
\t They treated us like cattle, not like human beings...
\t In the winter, all the villagers were driven out of their houses and convoyed to do labor. Our 
\t column stretched for several kilometers. The horror we had to go through cannot even be
\t imagined. My son Zhenya perished on the road from cold and hunger. 
\t Our route was marked with tiny graves like his. People died from the cold, hunger, the
\t constant beatings and the guards' bullets. We had to go through unspeakable horrors. I'm 25,
\t but I'm entirely gray. 
\t I lost everyone on that dreadful road: my son, my mother, my aunt, my sister. It was 
a miracle I survived.
\t \t Dear soldier. Kill those monsters without mercy. Give them not one minute of rest neither
\t day, nor night. Avenge our shame, avenge the blood of our children, mothers and sisters. 
\t \t \t \t \t \t \t \t \t With compliments and deep respect, Natalya Kolesova.
\n 
\t After this letter even the injured and lightly wounded decided to stay in service. The question of life and death, being decided now, concerns not only us, but our close ones as well.
\n 
\t SITUATION AND ORDER
\t Grossdeutschland Division and 11th Tank Division are flanking Novoselovka, Vladimirovka from two directions. The reconnaissance battalion of the Grossdeutschland in conjunction with tanks and assault guns advances on Novoselovka from the sector to the east of Verkhopenye village. 
\t Units of the 67th Guards Rifle Division withdraw to Novoselovka and Kalinovka under the pressure of superior enemy forces. Isolated units of the 3rd Mechanized Brigade, 1st Guards and 49th Tank Brigades, separate antitank rifle crews, and guns from the 35th antitank artillery regiment withdraw to the southern outskirts of Novoselovka as well. In the vicinity of Novoselovka, on the line between Hill 251.4, Hill 240.4, Sitnoe area, the 86th Independent Tank Brigade makes a stand.
\n 
\t OPERATION INSTRUCTIONS:
\t The reconnaissance regiment of the Grossdeutschland Division, supported with tanks and assault guns, advances in the direction of Novoselovka. Enemy tank and infantry actions are supported with air strikes and artillery fire. Small groups of collaborators and traitors, causing havoc, take part in the offensive along side the enemy. 
\t In order to stop enemy tank advancement to the deep rear, division command has decided that units will fight their way through to the next defensive line, where they will destroy enemy tanks and infantry. 
\t Lagutin's composite group under the cover of artillery fire will withdraw to the southern outskirts of Novoselovka, where in conjunction with the 86th Tank Brigade and KV tanks from the heavy tank regiment, his group will secure the area for defensive actions. Position the frontline 500m to the south of Novoselovka, create antitank strong points in the village itself and along the second trench line on the northwestern outskirts of Novoselovka. 
\t Concentrate your efforts on holding the center of the village. By any means prevent the enemy from reaching the northern outskirts of Novoselovka. If the enemy breaks through the first position, withdraw to Novoselovka and the second line under the cover of tanks and artillery fire.
\t If the enemy outflanks the defenses, counterattack him decisively with all available forces and do not allow him to reach our rear. 
\t The defensive positions' flanks are covered with previously deployed minefields. To deny the enemy the chance to remove the minefields, immediately assign emergency fire teams.
[Rus_Novoselovka.FullName]
Novoselovka 
[Rus_Kalinovka.Description]
\t July 10, morning. The Germans pushed us out of Novoselovka and attempted to advance further northward along the road to Oboyan, but were stopped by the tankers from the 10th Tank Corps. They are now looking for a weak spot in our defense, that's why we were dispatched to the right flank of the division at Kalinovka. To the south of the village, on Hill 232.8, we are digging trenches again. A trench or a pit is the only sanctuary and salvation from all the dangers of the war. It protects from bullets, shrapnel and even tank tracks, if the trench is deep enough. We do not dig because we're ordered to. It's done voluntarily, often spending our last bits of energy. Everyone knows that a trench should be deep enough. Otherwise it won't protect you. 
\t Yesterday in the vicinity of Novoselovka we saw our air force in action with our own eyes. The air strike was a breathtaking performance. The whole German frontline shuddered under its fiery rain. Machine guns, air defense guns of every caliber, rifles raised a sea of flame. Tracer streaks slashed the sky. Red, blue, green, foggy-white. The attack planes emerged from the clouds into enemy fire, but nothing could deflect them from their purpose. It looked like they despised death. They released their bombs. The bombs somersaulted at first, but then they recovered and coasted at the Germans with their speed increasing. Then the attack planes launched rockets, which looked like pencils from the ground. Screeching, they rushed at the ground, leaving fiery streaks. Powerful explosions enveloped the German troops. It looked like nothing remained alive there. But the Germans kept firing. One attack plane blew up in the air; another one fell to the ground with smoke in its wake.
\t The war leaves its bloody mark on the ground as well. On the way to Kalinovka we passed a field with shot up tanks. The thick metal tank armor was pierced with armor-piercing shells. Layers of steel were rent, rolled or twisted, like jagged petals of an unknown flower. Some tanks were black, those which had burned. There were blood stains on some of them, and occasionally we saw maimed human bodies nearby. The monstrous exhibition contrasted sharply with the stillness of the verdant field. And I know that this death memorial will soon disappear, it will be recast into new tanks and guns. New people will come, and they'll be dispatched into the chains of the war machine, that works unceasingly and asks for more and more sacrifices.
\n 
\t SITUATION AND ORDER
\t Several units of the Grossdeutschland Division are rallying on the Novoselovka sector. The main body prepares an attack in the west to the north of Verkhopenye and in the northwest towards Kalinovka. The reconnaissance battalion of the Grossdeutschland Division assaults Kalinovka. 
\t Units of the 67th Guards Rifle Division guard the line 1km north of Novoselovka, Hill 232.8, Kruglik grove. In the vicinity of Kochetovka units of the 309th Rifle Division assume the defensive. Brigades of the 10th Tank Corps approach Kalinovka.
\n 
\t OPERATION INSTRUCTIONS:
\t The reconnaissance regiment of the Grossdeutschland Division has deployed in the waiting area and is ready to advance towards Hill 232.8, Kalinovka. The arrival of reinforcements for the enemy strike force composed of tanks and assault guns has been spotted. The German offensive is supported with rocket artillery and ground attack aircraft. 
\t Lagutin's composite group will secure the defensive area on the southeastern slopes of Hill 232.8. It is reinforced with units from division's training battalion and an antitank gun battery. Isolated tanks of the 22nd Tank Brigade and infantry groups withdraw to the sector of the hill. With the help of your commanders, gather the tanks, the other small units and isolated soldiers withdrawing from the frontline without orders and organize them into battle formations. 
\t Prevent the enemy from advancing towards Kalinovka. Concentrate your efforts on holding the position before the ridge of the hill. Ensure the tank brigade of the 10th Tank Corps is deployed into combat in the vicinity of Hill 232.8. Cover your flanks by organizing optimal mounted machine gun and antitank positions. Prevent the enemy from passing your defenses. 
\t Upon arrival of the tank brigade from the 10th Tank Corps support its advancement with all types of weaponry. Establish communication with the arriving tanks, mark minefield gaps and the positioning of the troops under your command. Organize intelligence and mark targets for the tanks and artillery. In conjunction with the tankers execute a counterattack and push the Germans back to their starting positions. Support the counterattack with artillery and mounted machine gun fire. 
\t Ground attack aircraft will attack the enemy in your area.
[Rus_Kalinovka.FullName]
Kalinovka

